lesson_id = "sort_bubble"
order = 10
section = "Sorting"
unlocks_tower = "tower_bubble"
unlocks_level = 5
title = "Bubble Sort"
category = "data_structures"
icon_text = "↑↑"
prerequisites = Array[String](["ds_linked_lists"])
visualization_scene = ExtResource("2_10maa")
concept_text = "Bubble Sort is the simplest sorting algorithm. It walks through the list multiple times, comparing each PAIR of adjacent items and swapping them if they're in the wrong order. The largest values \"bubble up\" to the end like bubbles rising in water.

Imagine sorting 4 numbers: [5, 3, 8, 1]

Pass 1 — compare each pair:
- Is 5 > 3? YES → swap → [3, 5, 8, 1]
- Is 5 > 8? NO → no swap
- Is 8 > 1? YES → swap → [3, 5, 1, 8]

The 8 \"bubbled\" to the end. That's why it's called Bubble Sort.

Pass 2 — same thing, but we can skip the last position (8 is already in its final spot):
- Is 3 > 5? NO
- Is 5 > 1? YES → swap → [3, 1, 5, 8]

Pass 3 — skip the last two positions:
- Is 3 > 1? YES → swap → [1, 3, 5, 8]

Done! Sorted: [1, 3, 5, 8]

Key facts:
- SLOW for large lists: O(n²) — for 100 items, you might do 10,000 comparisons
- VERY slow if the data is in reverse order
- VERY fast if the data is already nearly sorted (O(n) — one pass, no swaps)
- EASY to write and understand — that's why teachers love it
- Not used in real-world software (there are much faster algorithms)
- Good for: tiny lists, learning, when simplicity matters more than speed"

real_world_example = "Imagine a line of students who need to be sorted by height (shortest to tallest). You walk down the line. If the person on the left is TALLER than the person on the right, you swap them. Keep walking.

The TALLEST person \"bubbles\" to the end of the line on the first pass, because every time you compare them to the person on their right, they're taller, so they swap — and they keep moving right until they reach the end.

    Heights: [165, 150, 172, 148]

    Pass 1: walk and swap if left > right
    [150, 165, 148, 172]  ← 172 bubbled to the end

    Pass 2: skip the last position (172 is in place)
    [150, 148, 165, 172]  ← 165 bubbled to second-to-last

    Pass 3: skip the last two positions
    [148, 150, 165, 172]  ← sorted!

Each pass guarantees that the next-tallest person reaches their correct spot at the right end. After n-1 passes, the whole line is sorted.

Another real-life example: organizing books on a shelf by height. You compare two adjacent books, swap if needed, keep going. Tallest book ends up on the right. Then you do another pass. Slow but it works.

A third example: sorting cards in your hand. The simplest way is bubble sort — compare two cards, swap if needed, slide the bigger one to the right. Keep doing this. It's slow, but if you only have 5-10 cards, who cares?"

guided_example = "Let's trace Bubble Sort on [64, 34, 25, 12], one pass at a time.

Pass 1 — Compare pairs (0-1, 1-2, 2-3). The LARGEST value ends up at index 3.

Step 1: Is 64 > 34? YES → swap. Array: [34, 64, 25, 12]
Step 2: Is 64 > 25? YES → swap. Array: [34, 25, 64, 12]
Step 3: Is 64 > 12? YES → swap. Array: [34, 25, 12, 64]

After pass 1: [34, 25, 12, 64]. The 64 is in its final position (index 3).

Pass 2 — Compare pairs (0-1, 1-2). We don't need to check position 3 anymore (64 is in place).

Step 1: Is 34 > 25? YES → swap. Array: [25, 34, 12, 64]
Step 2: Is 34 > 12? YES → swap. Array: [25, 12, 34, 64]

After pass 2: [25, 12, 34, 64]. The 34 is in its final position (index 2).

Pass 3 — Compare pair (0-1) only.

Step 1: Is 25 > 12? YES → swap. Array: [12, 25, 34, 64]

After pass 3: [12, 25, 34, 64]. SORTED!

Notice the pattern: after each pass, one more value is locked in at the right end. The largest value \"bubbles up\" to its final spot first.

Total: 3 passes, 6 comparisons, 6 swaps. For 4 items, that's the worst case but it's still small. For 100 items, you'd need 99 passes and roughly 10,000 comparisons — that's why Bubble Sort is slow for big lists."

recap_text = "- Bubble Sort compares adjacent pairs and swaps if they're in the wrong order
- After each pass, the largest remaining value \"bubbles\" to its final spot at the end
- Number of passes: n-1 for n items
- Time complexity: O(n²) on average and worst case
- Best case: O(n) if already sorted (one pass, no swaps)
- Space: O(1) — sorts in place, no extra memory needed
- Very simple to write and understand
- Too slow for real-world use on large data
- Great for: learning, tiny lists, nearly-sorted data
- Not great for: large lists, performance-critical code"

challenge_instruction = "Drag the blocks to sort [7, 3, 9, 2] using one pass of Bubble Sort. You should make 3 comparisons and possibly swap some."
challenge_blocks = Array[String](["arr = [7, 3, 9, 2]", "for j in range(3):", "    if arr[j] > arr[j+1]:", "        arr[j], arr[j+1] = arr[j+1], arr[j]", "print(arr)"])
correct_sequence = Array[String](["arr = [7, 3, 9, 2]", "for j in range(3):", "    if arr[j] > arr[j+1]:", "        arr[j], arr[j+1] = arr[j+1], arr[j]", "print(arr)"])
challenge_hint = "Loop 3 times (since we have 4 items, we compare 3 pairs). For each pair, check if left is bigger. If so, swap them in one line using arr[j], arr[j+1] = arr[j+1], arr[j]. Print the result."
code_template = "arr = [7, 3, 9, 2]
for j in range(___):
    if arr[j] > arr[j+1]:
        arr[j], arr[j+1] = ___, arr[j]
print(arr)"
challenge_code = "arr = [7, 3, 9, 2]
for j in range(3):
    if arr[j] > arr[j+1]:
        arr[j], arr[j+1] = arr[j+1], arr[j]
print(arr)"
expected_output = "[3, 7, 2, 9]"
practice_question = "What happens in ONE pass of Bubble Sort?"
practice_options = Array[String](["The smallest value moves to the start", "The largest value bubbles to the end", "The whole array gets sorted", "The array gets reversed"])
practice_correct_index = 1
practice_explanation = "Each pass compares adjacent pairs and swaps if out of order. The largest value keeps getting swapped to the right until it reaches the end. You need n-1 passes total to sort n items. The smallest value doesn't move anywhere special — it just slowly drifts toward the start as the larger values bubble past it."
